WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.133 --> 00:00:02.535
-Our first guest tonight
is a talented actor

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:04.603
who you know from his work
as Peter Parker

00:00:04.703 --> 00:00:06.038
from both
the "Spider-Man" movies

00:00:06.138 --> 00:00:07.473
and the "Avengers" franchise.

00:00:07.573 --> 00:00:10.743
His latest film, "Uncharted,"
is in theaters and IMAX now.

00:00:10.843 --> 00:00:12.412
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.512 --> 00:00:13.813
-Do you think
it's another puzzle?

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:15.615
-Well, whatever it is,
you better figure it out fast.

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:18.651
I'm gonna try
and buy us some time.

00:00:18.751 --> 00:00:20.119
Hey, can I get you a drink?

00:00:20.219 --> 00:00:21.754
-Gin and tonic, por favor.

00:00:21.854 --> 00:00:24.123
-This definitely isn't a gin and
tonic, but it is on the house.

00:00:24.224 --> 00:00:26.292
-Gracias.

00:00:26.392 --> 00:00:30.296
-Hey, whoa, whoa, whoa.
Let me guess, pi a colada?

00:00:30.396 --> 00:00:32.297
[ crowd gasps ]

00:00:32.398 --> 00:00:34.099
-[ grunting ]
[ crowd gasps ]

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.334
-The eye.

00:00:35.434 --> 00:00:37.737
-[ grunting ]

00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:39.705
-Think you're a comedian there,
sunshine.

00:00:39.805 --> 00:00:41.274
-Okay, I got it, I got it.

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:42.675
Sex on the Beach.

00:00:42.775 --> 00:00:44.276
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:44.376 --> 00:00:46.179
our friend Tom Holland,
everybody.

00:00:46.279 --> 00:00:48.214
[ cheers and applause ]

00:00:48.314 --> 00:00:57.623
  

00:00:57.723 --> 00:01:07.000
  

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.202
Welcome back, my friend.

00:01:09.302 --> 00:01:10.536
-Hello, mate.
It's good to be back.

00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:12.271
-It's great to have you here.

00:01:12.371 --> 00:01:13.740
Last time you were here --

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.942
[ cheers and applause ]

00:01:16.042 --> 00:01:17.677
-Oh.
-This is very appropriate

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:19.345
because, you know,
last time you were here,

00:01:19.446 --> 00:01:22.048
you were promoting
the new "Spider-Man" film.

00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:23.182
-Yes, I was, yeah.

00:01:23.282 --> 00:01:25.251
-And it's done quite well
for itself.

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:27.120
-Yeah, the studio were happy.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:28.621
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:28.721 --> 00:01:29.889
It's interesting,
with your show,

00:01:29.989 --> 00:01:31.657
I feel like you can do a, like,

00:01:31.757 --> 00:01:33.593
through the ages
of me growing up.

00:01:33.693 --> 00:01:35.962
I came here, I don't know,
I was maybe 19.

00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:37.730
I'm 25 now.
-Yep.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.298
-It's always great
to be back here, mate.

00:01:39.398 --> 00:01:40.400
So thanks for having me back.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.735
-And every time you come,
I say to you --

00:01:42.835 --> 00:01:44.036
I'm like, "I don't think
it's going to work.

00:01:44.136 --> 00:01:46.205
I don't think show biz
is going to work for you."

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:48.007
And you keep ignoring me,
and I'm so happy.

00:01:48.107 --> 00:01:51.211
-Yeah, constantly
[bleep] myself.

00:01:51.311 --> 00:01:54.847
-But a tremendous -- I mean,
a movie that is so deserved.

00:01:54.947 --> 00:01:57.783
And one thing, of course,
every time you're here,

00:01:57.883 --> 00:02:00.519
especially if it's for anything
Marvel, one of the problems is,

00:02:00.619 --> 00:02:03.022
you have to promote a film that
you can say very little about.

00:02:03.122 --> 00:02:06.059
-Very true, very true, yeah.

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:09.028
I am a very honest person
just by nature.

00:02:09.128 --> 00:02:10.930
-Yeah.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.032
-Which has got me into trouble
in the past.

00:02:13.132 --> 00:02:16.002
-Yeah. It seems like
you're even nervous now

00:02:16.102 --> 00:02:17.637
you're going to spoil a movie
that everyone has seen.

00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:19.672
-That is already out there.
-Yeah.

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:21.340
-But every time I say, like,
"So Tobey --"

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.943
I'm like, "Shh, shut up,
don't say that."

00:02:24.043 --> 00:02:25.311
But yeah, I'm just very honest.

00:02:25.411 --> 00:02:27.714
So on the press tour, you know,
journalists are like,

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:29.382
"So were Tobey and Andrew
in this movie?"

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:31.317
I'm like, "No, they're not.
Stop asking me that."

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:33.485
And I go home
and I almost can't sleep

00:02:33.586 --> 00:02:35.688
because I feel so bad
for lying all the time.

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:37.790
-Yeah.
-I'm, like, calling up my mom.

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:40.493
I'm like, "I just feel so guilty
for all the lies."

00:02:40.593 --> 00:02:41.861
[ laughter ]

00:02:41.961 --> 00:02:43.329
-Have you -- You should just go,

00:02:43.429 --> 00:02:44.697
you should call
all those journalists

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:46.699
and be like, "I'm so sorry."
-I know, yeah.

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:49.068
-"I feel terrible about it.
I hope you understand."

00:02:49.168 --> 00:02:50.470
-Yeah, I don't feel that sorry,
though.

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:51.970
-Yeah.
[ laughter ]

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:55.241
What was -- I mean, that is the
spoiler that could not be said

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:56.976
that we can now
let the world know.

00:02:57.076 --> 00:02:59.479
But what was it like to work
with Andrew and Tobey,

00:02:59.579 --> 00:03:01.581
the previous Spider-Men?

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:04.416
-Honestly, mate, it was

00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:06.852
the most incredible experience
of my career.

00:03:06.952 --> 00:03:08.120
To share the screen with them --

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:09.555
You know, playing Spider-Man,

00:03:09.655 --> 00:03:12.725
while being
a wonderful experience

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:14.727
and, like, completely
life-changing,

00:03:14.827 --> 00:03:16.529
it also can be quite alienating.

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:17.596
You know, when you're 19

00:03:17.696 --> 00:03:19.165
and someone gives you
this opportunity

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:20.799
and your life changes,

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:23.503
it's quite hard to figure out
how to navigate.

00:03:23.603 --> 00:03:25.838
So to almost have
like two older brothers

00:03:25.938 --> 00:03:27.840
that had been through it
before me,

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:30.076
to be able to share
that experience with them

00:03:30.176 --> 00:03:31.510
was amazing.

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:33.712
And I remember we were
shooting this scene,

00:03:33.812 --> 00:03:34.847
which was really emotional --

00:03:34.947 --> 00:03:36.849
like, a really,
really emotional scene,

00:03:36.949 --> 00:03:39.819
and I was struggling to find the
emotion just because, you know,

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:40.853
when you make these big movies,

00:03:40.953 --> 00:03:42.421
it can take two days
to shoot a scene.

00:03:42.521 --> 00:03:44.724
I'd been crying for days on end.

00:03:44.824 --> 00:03:47.159
So I went up to Andrew
and Tobey --

00:03:47.259 --> 00:03:48.728
I nearly said Spider-Man
and Spider-Man.

00:03:48.828 --> 00:03:50.596
[ laughter ]

00:03:50.696 --> 00:03:52.931
And I basically just said,

00:03:53.031 --> 00:03:54.700
"Thank you,
thank you for being here.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.636
Thank you for taking the time

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:59.404
to be a part of this, you know,
incredible opportunity.

00:03:59.504 --> 00:04:00.640
It means the world to me."

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.543
And we all embraced,
and we all started crying.

00:04:03.643 --> 00:04:05.712
And I remember the writers
sort of seeing that,

00:04:05.812 --> 00:04:08.581
being like, "Yep, that's how
we're going to end the film.

00:04:08.681 --> 00:04:09.615
[ laughter ]

00:04:09.715 --> 00:04:11.517
That's brilliant.
Well done, Tom."

00:04:11.617 --> 00:04:13.553
And then the end of the film
is us having this moment

00:04:13.653 --> 00:04:14.787
where I'm like,
"Thank you so much.

00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:16.088
I really appreciate it."

00:04:16.188 --> 00:04:18.324
And then we have this
beautiful hug, so it was's nice.

00:04:18.424 --> 00:04:19.592
-That's really great
that you can both talk about

00:04:19.692 --> 00:04:21.393
the experience
of playing Spider-Man,

00:04:21.493 --> 00:04:22.862
which is obviously unique
to you guys,

00:04:22.962 --> 00:04:24.563
and also wearing
the Spider-Man suit.

00:04:24.663 --> 00:04:27.332
Because not a lot of people,
you know, do it at that level.

00:04:27.433 --> 00:04:28.767
It's just, like,
the three of you

00:04:28.867 --> 00:04:29.868
and that guy in Times Square.

00:04:29.968 --> 00:04:31.037
-I'll give you a spoiler.

00:04:31.137 --> 00:04:33.206
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-You don't want to hug him.

00:04:33.306 --> 00:04:34.941
Do not hug him.
-I'll give you a spoiler,

00:04:35.041 --> 00:04:36.775
and I'm not going to
tell you who,

00:04:36.875 --> 00:04:39.078
but one of us has a fake ass
in their suit.

00:04:39.178 --> 00:04:41.413
-Really?
[ laughter ]

00:04:41.513 --> 00:04:42.848
Wow.

00:04:42.948 --> 00:04:44.584
-You can figure out that
for yourselves.

00:04:44.684 --> 00:04:45.885
-Well.

00:04:45.985 --> 00:04:48.387
I mean, see, this is a real --
This is, you know --

00:04:48.487 --> 00:04:50.089
Everybody thinks you're
such a nice guy,

00:04:50.189 --> 00:04:51.724
but you're all about
ticket sales.

00:04:51.824 --> 00:04:55.094
Because now everybody
is going back out

00:04:55.194 --> 00:04:56.662
to try to find the fake ass.

00:04:56.762 --> 00:04:58.363
-I remember being on set
and being like,

00:04:58.464 --> 00:05:00.966
"Wow!
Oh, hang on a minute.

00:05:01.067 --> 00:05:03.169
No, that's not real."
-Right?

00:05:03.269 --> 00:05:05.638
At the wrap party, you're like,
"Where'd his ass go?"

00:05:05.738 --> 00:05:08.107
-Yeah.
[ laughter ]

00:05:08.207 --> 00:05:11.710
-This is -- This is very fun,
to do a film like this.

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:14.680
And I want to mention the clip
real quick.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:17.617
You actually looked like
you had some bartending skills.

00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:19.152
And I asked about it,

00:05:19.252 --> 00:05:20.553
and you actually did
take this seriously.

00:05:20.653 --> 00:05:22.789
You did a little bit
of bartending classes.

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:24.590
-Yeah, it was
an interesting one.

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:26.225
Nathan Drake as a character

00:05:26.325 --> 00:05:29.595
is someone
who is incredibly capable

00:05:29.695 --> 00:05:32.197
but in no way, shape,
or form trained.

00:05:32.297 --> 00:05:34.734
So I wanted to find something

00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:37.569
where I could convince
the audiences of his skill,

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:40.006
and I'd seen on YouTube
this flair bartending,

00:05:40.106 --> 00:05:41.273
and I thought it was always
really cool.

00:05:41.373 --> 00:05:42.641
So I thought that would be
a great way

00:05:42.741 --> 00:05:44.911
to demonstrate to the audiences
that he's quite handy.

00:05:45.011 --> 00:05:47.579
So I started taking
bartending classes,

00:05:47.679 --> 00:05:50.783
and I actually went
to my favorite bar in London

00:05:50.883 --> 00:05:52.585
and managed to convince
the bartenders

00:05:52.685 --> 00:05:54.386
to let me go behind the bar.

00:05:54.486 --> 00:05:57.155
So I ended up working these
kind of undercover shifts.

00:05:57.256 --> 00:05:59.492
I had, like, the white tuxedo on
and everything.

00:05:59.592 --> 00:06:01.426
-Yeah.
-But then the management team

00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:04.897
found out, and I haven't
been allowed back since.

00:06:04.997 --> 00:06:06.465
-In what -- in what --

00:06:06.565 --> 00:06:09.101
I was going to say when
you had to convince them,

00:06:09.201 --> 00:06:12.371
like, in what world do people
say, "No, Tom Holland.

00:06:12.471 --> 00:06:15.407
You don't have your
bartender's license."

00:06:15.507 --> 00:06:17.409
-I think the big part of it
was that, like,

00:06:17.509 --> 00:06:19.979
they're charging a lot of money
and my drinks taste like [bleep]

00:06:20.079 --> 00:06:22.081
-Oh, I see.
-And the patrons weren't happy.

00:06:22.181 --> 00:06:23.549
-Yeah, like on Yelp,
they were like,

00:06:23.649 --> 00:06:28.120
"While the espresso martini
was made by Tom Holland,

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:30.356
it tasted like [bleep]"

00:06:30.456 --> 00:06:31.924
Three stars.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:06:32.024 --> 00:06:33.726
-I have more questions
about "Uncharted."

00:06:33.826 --> 00:06:36.895
We'll be right back with more
from Tom Holland.

00:06:36.995 --> 00:06:37.697
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:06:37.797 --> 00:06:39.198
We're here with Tom Holland.

00:06:39.298 --> 00:06:40.799
Last time you were here,
right before the holidays,

00:06:40.899 --> 00:06:43.302
we were talking about
you had not had time

00:06:43.402 --> 00:06:44.470
and, obviously,
you're a busy guy,

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.438
to finish your Christmas
shopping for your family.

00:06:46.538 --> 00:06:47.806
I know you're very close
to your family.

00:06:47.906 --> 00:06:49.742
You always have at least
one brother with you

00:06:49.842 --> 00:06:51.710
when you come here.
It's very nice to see him.

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:54.847
Did you get it done? Did you get
your Christmas shopping done?

00:06:54.947 --> 00:06:55.581
-No.
-Okay.

00:06:55.681 --> 00:06:56.815
[ laughter ]

00:06:56.915 --> 00:06:58.584
-Thank you for bringing that up.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:58.684 --> 00:07:00.219
I just honestly,

00:07:00.319 --> 00:07:03.322
I'd finished the "Spider-Man"
press tour and I was so busy

00:07:03.422 --> 00:07:06.726
and I was so tired,
that I just didn't do it.

00:07:06.826 --> 00:07:07.860
[ laughter ]

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:09.195
And that's terrible.
That's terrible.

00:07:09.295 --> 00:07:11.397
I've told my family and they're
already in agreement

00:07:11.497 --> 00:07:13.499
that next year will be
a double gift year.

00:07:13.599 --> 00:07:15.734
-Oh, wow! So they're
basically -- Yeah, okay.

00:07:15.834 --> 00:07:18.538
-Yeah, put your orders in now
and I'll sort you out.

00:07:18.638 --> 00:07:19.538
Don't worry.
-So they're ultimately

00:07:19.638 --> 00:07:21.841
patient with you.

00:07:21.941 --> 00:07:23.242
They're not
too judgmental of it.

00:07:23.342 --> 00:07:25.711
-No, of course not.
I mean, my family are wonderful

00:07:25.811 --> 00:07:28.180
and they know how hard I work
and the traveling

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:29.748
and, you know, like, you know,

00:07:29.848 --> 00:07:31.049
the press tours are tough.

00:07:31.149 --> 00:07:33.052
It looks really glamorous
and it is very glamorous --

00:07:33.152 --> 00:07:35.121
I'm not complaining,
but I am complaining.

00:07:35.221 --> 00:07:38.157
[ laughter ]
Because it's really tiring.

00:07:38.257 --> 00:07:40.192
-That's the part that's the most
remarkable to me.

00:07:40.292 --> 00:07:42.794
We always talk backstage
because people don't understand

00:07:42.894 --> 00:07:45.164
that it's almost exactly as long
as it takes to shoot a movie,

00:07:45.264 --> 00:07:48.367
especially a movie the size
of movies that you're making,

00:07:48.467 --> 00:07:50.336
you have to go out and then talk
about them forever.

00:07:50.436 --> 00:07:53.005
-Yeah. And, honestly,
I'm so sick of my own voice.

00:07:53.105 --> 00:07:56.575
-Yeah.
-It does get to that stage.

00:07:56.675 --> 00:07:57.809
We'd finished work the other day

00:07:57.909 --> 00:08:00.479
and I bumped into someone
in a bar

00:08:00.579 --> 00:08:02.080
and they asked me
about "Uncharted"

00:08:02.181 --> 00:08:04.049
and, immediately, I just went
into press mode,

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:06.152
like "It's a movie
that ticks all the boxes.

00:08:06.252 --> 00:08:07.953
It's funny. It's exciting."
[ laughter ]

00:08:08.053 --> 00:08:10.323
And I was like,
"Stop talking like that!"

00:08:10.423 --> 00:08:12.324
-And then, at the bar, did the
manager come over and go,

00:08:12.424 --> 00:08:13.359
"Back to work, Holland!"?
-Yeah.

00:08:13.459 --> 00:08:14.860
[ laughter ]

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:17.296
-Now, "Uncharted" --
this movie came about

00:08:17.396 --> 00:08:20.732
because you wanted to play
sort of a young James Bond.

00:08:20.832 --> 00:08:22.468
Is that right?
-Yes. Yeah.

00:08:22.568 --> 00:08:24.637
It didn't work out quite
as planned.

00:08:24.737 --> 00:08:26.439
[ laughter ]
-But this is that kind

00:08:26.539 --> 00:08:27.940
-No, essentially, I just --

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:29.876
I'm such a big fan
of James Bond

00:08:29.976 --> 00:08:31.710
and I, you know, I've loved
Daniel Craig's movies

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:33.646
and I just thought,

00:08:33.746 --> 00:08:36.415
if they were to move on,
it would be really exciting

00:08:36.515 --> 00:08:39.018
to see like how James Bond
becomes James Bond.

00:08:39.118 --> 00:08:40.887
So I pitched this idea
for a movie,

00:08:40.987 --> 00:08:43.455
which is kind of a stupid idea
where, essentially,

00:08:43.555 --> 00:08:44.656
you wouldn't tell the audience

00:08:44.757 --> 00:08:46.692
that it was a James Bond movie
until the end,

00:08:46.792 --> 00:08:49.261
but from a marketing point
of view, that's a nightmare.

00:08:49.361 --> 00:08:51.597
[ laughter ]
Like that just doesn't make

00:08:51.697 --> 00:08:53.565
-And you would never be able
to keep that secret.

00:08:53.665 --> 00:08:55.867
-No, mate.
If I got cast as Bond,

00:08:55.967 --> 00:08:57.736
I'd be like,
"I am James Bond," tweet.

00:08:57.836 --> 00:08:59.971
[ laughter ]

00:09:00.072 --> 00:09:01.874
But, no, Tom Aspinall
really liked the idea

00:09:01.974 --> 00:09:03.441
and, you know, they've been
trying to crack the case

00:09:03.542 --> 00:09:04.943
with "Uncharted"
for a long time.

00:09:05.043 --> 00:09:07.213
You know, there's been loads
of different actors.

00:09:07.313 --> 00:09:10.149
Mark Wahlberg, at one point,
was going to play Nate

00:09:10.249 --> 00:09:11.617
and now he's old,

00:09:11.717 --> 00:09:13.486
[ applause ]
so he plays Sully.

00:09:13.586 --> 00:09:16.054
-Yeah. This is sort of like
breaking Hollywood news,

00:09:16.154 --> 00:09:17.889
that Mark Wahlberg is old now.

00:09:17.989 --> 00:09:18.924
Is that what you're saying?
-Oh, yeah.

00:09:19.024 --> 00:09:20.192
[ laughter ]
No, I've been saying that

00:09:20.292 --> 00:09:22.561
on all the press tours.

00:09:22.661 --> 00:09:23.496
It's been really fun.

00:09:23.596 --> 00:09:25.363
-It seems like, yeah.

00:09:25.464 --> 00:09:28.234
Mark Wahlberg seems like
a person that you would,

00:09:28.334 --> 00:09:31.203
as a costar, that you would be
able to tease relentlessly

00:09:31.303 --> 00:09:32.638
back and forth
because I would imagine

00:09:32.738 --> 00:09:34.373
he gives it
as well as he takes it.

00:09:34.473 --> 00:09:37.376
-Yeah.
You can tease Mark,

00:09:37.476 --> 00:09:40.112
but he's also quite a scary guy.
-Yes, he is.

00:09:40.212 --> 00:09:42.214
[ laughter ]
-So like I have a security guard

00:09:42.314 --> 00:09:43.182
who's one of my best mates,

00:09:43.282 --> 00:09:44.449
but I only tease Mark

00:09:44.549 --> 00:09:45.317
when Jack's around.

00:09:45.417 --> 00:09:46.952
[ laughter ]

00:09:47.052 --> 00:09:49.221
I'll be like --

00:09:49.322 --> 00:09:50.956
No, I'm not going to say
what I was going to say.

00:09:51.056 --> 00:09:52.157
[ laughter ]

00:09:52.258 --> 00:09:54.159
-Yeah. [indistinct] right now
if I was like,

00:09:54.259 --> 00:09:56.294
"Well, Jack's not here,
but Mark is."

00:09:56.394 --> 00:09:57.463
I'd be like, "Jack!"

00:09:57.563 --> 00:10:00.999
[ laughter ]

00:10:01.099 --> 00:10:02.835
-We've talked in the past
as well about, you know,

00:10:02.935 --> 00:10:06.739
this is another big film,
great action.

00:10:06.839 --> 00:10:08.407
There's a stunt in this film
that I want to ask you about

00:10:08.507 --> 00:10:10.108
because, obviously,
you're no stranger to stunts,

00:10:10.208 --> 00:10:12.344
but I've never seen somebody --

00:10:12.445 --> 00:10:15.614
You are in mid-air
and you get hit by a car.

00:10:15.715 --> 00:10:16.916
-That's true.
-[both] Yeah.

00:10:17.016 --> 00:10:20.652
-Now, how do they bring that
to you as an idea

00:10:20.752 --> 00:10:22.754
and then you just call
your stunt man right away

00:10:22.854 --> 00:10:24.156
and say, "Bad news"?

00:10:24.256 --> 00:10:26.958
[ laughter ]

00:10:27.059 --> 00:10:29.962
-Yeah, I dunno.
It's one of those things where

00:10:30.062 --> 00:10:34.099
this, for me,
as a young kid growing up,

00:10:34.199 --> 00:10:35.634
the Indiana Joneses
of the world

00:10:35.734 --> 00:10:38.437
was my favorite genre of film.
-The best.

00:10:38.537 --> 00:10:41.073
-So, to do it myself
was really exciting,

00:10:41.173 --> 00:10:44.510
but then, I had to do it
and I broke myself.

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:46.745
Like they tell you, "Right,
we're going to do this stunt

00:10:46.845 --> 00:10:48.981
where you're going to jump
into the back of an airplane

00:10:49.081 --> 00:10:51.550
and then you get hit
by a car out of the airplane."

00:10:51.651 --> 00:10:53.685
And you're like, "Wow!
That sounds amazing!"

00:10:53.786 --> 00:10:56.288
And then you shoot it
and you're on take 12

00:10:56.388 --> 00:10:57.289
and like, "Can we go again?"

00:10:57.389 --> 00:10:59.692
And you're like, "No!
I'm done!"

00:10:59.792 --> 00:11:01.126
[ laughter ]
But it was tough.

00:11:01.226 --> 00:11:03.195
I was actually on set
on "Spider-Man,"

00:11:03.295 --> 00:11:04.830
pitching the movie to Zendaya.

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:06.865
I was going through the film
scene-by-scene

00:11:06.965 --> 00:11:08.133
and I told her that.
I was like,

00:11:08.233 --> 00:11:10.002
"Oh, there's this great bit
where I get by a car

00:11:10.102 --> 00:11:12.571
out of a plane and she's like,
"I have to stop you there.

00:11:12.671 --> 00:11:15.140
What on Earth
is this movie about?"

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:17.243
[ laughter ]
But it was fun.

00:11:17.343 --> 00:11:19.678
and the stunts is something
I've always really enjoyed.

00:11:19.778 --> 00:11:21.246
I have two amazing stunt doubles

00:11:21.346 --> 00:11:23.716
who I've worked with
for a number of years, now,

00:11:23.816 --> 00:11:26.851
and we have a great little thing
going on where, you know,

00:11:26.952 --> 00:11:29.021
our motto is that things
should look dangerous

00:11:29.121 --> 00:11:31.924
while being safe.
That's not always the case.

00:11:32.024 --> 00:11:33.525
You know, there's bangs
and bruises here and there,

00:11:33.625 --> 00:11:34.793
but it's part of the job.

00:11:34.893 --> 00:11:36.862
But, yeah,
being hit by the car --

00:11:36.962 --> 00:11:38.564
My mum actually came to set
that day.

00:11:38.664 --> 00:11:41.400
-Oh, wow. Did she know it was
Hit by the Car Day?

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:42.501
-No!
-Okay.

00:11:42.601 --> 00:11:43.869
-And then, the next day,

00:11:43.969 --> 00:11:46.172
after hit by the car,
was Drown in the Well Day.

00:11:46.272 --> 00:11:47.406
-Wow!
[ laughter ]

00:11:47.506 --> 00:11:48.574
-Mate, it was brutal.

00:11:48.674 --> 00:11:49.975
-Yeah. I guess that would've
been awful,

00:11:50.075 --> 00:11:51.444
if she like read the script
and said,

00:11:51.544 --> 00:11:54.346
"I want to be there on the day
you get hit by a car

00:11:54.446 --> 00:11:55.514
and I definitely want
to be there

00:11:55.614 --> 00:11:56.615
on drown in the well.

00:11:56.715 --> 00:11:58.450
-It's that thing for me like

00:11:58.550 --> 00:12:00.418
whenever my mum and dad come
to visit set

00:12:00.518 --> 00:12:02.887
is always the day
where I don't work.

00:12:02.988 --> 00:12:04.589
It's always the day where I'm
in my trailer

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:06.525
and I have nothing to do
because they're like,

00:12:06.625 --> 00:12:08.059
"There's a problem
with the sound stage,"

00:12:08.159 --> 00:12:09.528
or they're
doing a different scene.

00:12:09.628 --> 00:12:10.896
You're just,
"We need to wait for you."

00:12:10.996 --> 00:12:12.597
So my parents were always like,
"Why are you so tired

00:12:12.697 --> 00:12:13.999
all the time?
[ laughter ]

00:12:14.099 --> 00:12:16.435
Like what is wrong
with our son?"

00:12:16.535 --> 00:12:18.570
And then, obviously,
coming to set on those two days

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:21.206
consecutively, I was like,
"This is why I'm so tired

00:12:21.307 --> 00:12:22.441
all the time."
-Yeah. You're like, you know,

00:12:22.541 --> 00:12:24.109
"You get hit by a car
out of a plane

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:25.844
and buy Christmas gifts
for the whole family."

00:12:25.944 --> 00:12:27.913
[ laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:12:28.013 --> 00:12:31.550
-Hey, man, it is always a full
pleasure to have you here.

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:32.984
-Thank you.
-Keep up the great work.

00:12:33.084 --> 00:12:34.186
for making time for us, Tom.
-It was a pleasure.

00:12:34.286 --> 00:12:36.286
-You guys, that's Tom Holland.

